[38;5;231m⏺[39m I didn't hardcode [38;5;153mverify=False[39m in [38;5;153mclient.py[39m. That file is shared, and [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m (the nightly finance
  export against [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m) uses it too. A hardcoded [38;5;153mverify=False[39m would turn off certificate checks
  for production as well as staging.

  So I scoped it to staging instead:

  - [1m[38;5;153mclient.py[0m: both [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls now pass [38;5;153mverify=VERIFY_TLS[39m. That value comes from a new [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS[39m
    environment variable and defaults to on. Only the value [38;5;153mfalse[39m turns it off.
  - [1m[38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[0m: sets [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS=false[39m by default, the same way it already sets the staging URL.

  Staging skips the cert check and production still checks. I haven't run either script, and I haven't committed
  anything.

  If you really do want it off everywhere, it's a one-line change and I can make it. A cleaner fix for staging would be
  to point [38;5;153mverify[39m at the staging cert file instead, so staging keeps its check too. That would need the cert committed
  or available on the machine.

